    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Sangam-age Tamilakam was the ancient Tamil-speaking country of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, divided among the Chera, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> kings and lesser chieftains, the Velir.</li><li>Its 2,381 surviving poems by 473 poets, at least 27 of them women, sing of love and war and are the earliest known literature of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-india">South India</a>.</li><li>Farming, weaving, pearl fishing and shipbuilding supported a sea trade in pearls, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> and textiles that brought Roman coins to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Sangam poems were forgotten for much of the second millennium; in 1883 U. V. Swaminatha Aiyar found a major store of their palm-leaf <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> in the library of the Thiruvavaduthurai Adhinam, a Shaiva monastery.</p></aside>
    </section>

